\f0\fs28\b Yamaha Soundbar (umbrella driver)\b0\fs20\par
\par
DriverWorks driver for Yamaha soundbars built on the Linkplay platform. Currently supports the \b YAS-209\b0 . Presents to Control4 as a \b Receiver\b0  proxy: power, volume, mute, input select and surround mode.\par
\par
\fs24\b Control Method\b0\fs20\par
\par
\bullet  \b IR\b0  \endash  the standard method, and the default. Works out of the box with an IR emitter. No network and no configuration beyond binding the emitter.\par
\bullet  \b IP - See Documentation for Usage\b0  \endash  adds state feedback and exact control, but has a caveat worth understanding before selecting it. See \b IP Control\b0  below.\par
\par
There is no Serial option: the soundbar has no serial port.\par
\par
\fs24\b IR Control\b0\fs20\par
\par
Bind a controller IR output to the driver's \b IR\b0  connection and place the emitter over the soundbar's IR window.\par
\par
\bullet  All IR codes were captured from the genuine YAS-209 remote, so they are correct for this model.\par
\bullet  Power, volume, mute, all four inputs and all six sound programs are available over IR.\par
\bullet  The three DSP switches (3D Surround, Clear Voice, Bass Extension) have \b toggle-only\b0  codes on the remote, so the driver can flip them but cannot set them to a specific state.\par
\bullet  \b IR is one-way.\b0  The driver receives nothing back, so it knows only what it last sent. Volume in particular is estimated and will drift if the soundbar is changed by anything else.\par
\bullet  Use \b Test IR Emitter\b0  during installation. It fires a single code with nothing else in the path and reports what Director believes is bound to the IR output. If the emitter does not blink, the fault is the binding, the emitter or the controller port \endash  not the code.\par
\par
\fs24\b IP Control\b0\fs20\par
\par
The soundbar's local API requires a client certificate for mutual TLS. That certificate was extracted from the Yamaha mobile app; it is a single shared Linkplay credential rather than anything issued to an individual soundbar, so it is expected to work on any unit of this model. It ships with the driver, so no setup is needed beyond entering the IP address. \b Be aware that a soundbar firmware update could stop this certificate being accepted, which would end IP control on that unit.\b0  The driver cannot repair itself if that happens \endash  replacing the certificate means rebuilding the driver \endash  so switch Control Method back to IR, which is unaffected.\par
\par
\bullet  \b Test IP Control Compatibility\b0  (Action) \endash  run this first, and again after any soundbar firmware update. It attempts the mutual-TLS handshake and a status read, then reports \b COMPATIBLE\b0  or \b NOT COMPATIBLE\b0  in the Lua output window, with the model and firmware the soundbar reported. Safe to run with Control Method set to IR; it only tests whether IP control could work.\par
\bullet  \b State feedback\b0  \endash  IP only. Power, input, volume, mute, sound program and the DSP settings are read back from the soundbar, so the Control4 UI stays correct when someone uses the Yamaha remote, the front panel or the app. None of this is possible over IR.\par
\bullet  \b Exact volume\b0  \endash  IP only. Volume is set to an absolute level instead of being estimated by pulsing.\par
\bullet  \b 3D Surround / Clear Voice / Bass Extension\b0  (properties) \endash  IP sets these to the exact state requested. Over IR the driver can only toggle them.\par
\bullet  \b Subwoofer Boost\b0  (property, -4 to +4) \endash  works over both methods, but only IP confirms the value that actually applied.\par
\bullet  \b Get Device Status\b0  (Action) \endash  reads everything available immediately and prints it, so there is no need to shorten the poll interval just to see current values.\par
\bullet  \b Poll Interval Seconds\b0  and \b State Poll Seconds\b0  \endash  IP only. Volume and mute are read often and cheaply; power, input and settings are read on the slower timer because each of those is a full TLS handshake. Set State Poll Seconds to 0 to disable state feedback and use Get Device Status instead.\par
\par
\fs24\b Actions\b0\fs20\par
\par
\bullet  \b Refresh Now\b0  \endash  immediate volume/mute read.\par
\bullet  \b Get Device Status\b0  \endash  reads and prints everything the driver can see.\par
\bullet  \b Test IR Emitter\b0  \endash  fires one IR code and reports the IR binding state.\par
\bullet  \b Test IP Control Compatibility\b0  \endash  verifies this soundbar accepts the driver's certificate.\par
\par
All four print to the Lua output window regardless of the Log Mode setting, so diagnostics are never hidden by a logging preference.\par
\par
\fs24\b Setup\b0\fs20\par
\par
1. Add the driver and bind the Receiver connection to the room.\par
2. \b For IR:\b0  bind a controller IR output to the IR connection, place the emitter, then run \b Test IR Emitter\b0 .\par
3. \b For IP:\b0  set \b IP Address\b0  (use a DHCP reservation), change \b Control Method\b0  to the IP option, then run \b Test IP Control Compatibility\b0 .\par
4. In Connections, bind the audio output to the room and wire real sources to the HDMI, TV, Bluetooth and Network inputs.\par
\par
\fs24\b Troubleshooting\b0\fs20\par
\par
\bullet  \b Nothing happens over IR\b0  \endash  run Test IR Emitter. No blink means the binding, the emitter or the controller IR port, not the codes.\par
\bullet  \b IP control stopped working\b0  \endash  run Test IP Control Compatibility. NOT COMPATIBLE after a soundbar firmware update means the certificate is no longer accepted; use IR.\par
\bullet  \b Values look stale\b0  \endash  state feedback requires Control Method = IP. Under IR the driver reports what it last sent, not what the soundbar is doing.\par
\bullet  \b Re-adding the driver resets every property\b0 , including IP Address. If something breaks immediately after an update, check the properties before the code.\par
\par
